  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glucagonoma is an uncommon pancreatic islet cell tumor originating from alpha cells, which secrete glucagon. They occasionally arise in extra-pancreatic locations (i.e., liver). Glucagonomas may secrete other hormones, such as gastrin, pancreatic polypeptide, and (rarely) insulin.",
              "pdf_page": 650,
              "printed_page": 568,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Excess circulating glucagon activates glyco genolysis and gluconeogenesis and inhibits glycogenesis. This increases protein catabolism and lipolysis. Glucagon excess can inhibit erythropoiesis. The culmination of these biochemical changes results in hyperglycemia and occasionally diabetes mellitus (DM), hypoaminoacidemia, anemia, and weight loss. Glucagon can exert a secretory effect on the small intestine, leading to diarrhea.",
              "pdf_page": 650,
              "printed_page": 568,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glucagonomas may cause an overall decrease in plasma amino acid concentrations, with epidermal protein depletion in the skin, leading to the classic dermatopathy associated with glucagonoma (necrolytic migratory erythema [NME]).",
              "pdf_page": 650,
              "printed_page": 568,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glucagonomas can affect numerous organ systems, including musculoskeletal, integumentary, endocrine, gastrointestinal, nervous/behavioral, and hepatobiliary.",
              "pdf_page": 650,
              "printed_page": 568,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
